<p>The biggest decision after "where" is "how". All-inclusive resorts dominate the Caribbean and parts of the Pacific, but they aren't automatically the best value or the best experience. Here's a straight way to decide.</p>

<h2 id="options">The three options</h2>
<h3>All-inclusive resort</h3>
<p>One price covers your room, meals, drinks and usually non-motorised water sports and entertainment. <b>Best for:</b> families with young children, groups who want a fixed budget, and anyone who mainly wants to relax by the pool and the sea. <b>Weak spot:</b> you see little of the real destination, and food quality varies a lot between brands and price tiers.</p>
<h3>Hotel (room only or with breakfast)</h3>
<p>Boutique and city-style hotels put you in the middle of town. <b>Best for:</b> couples and food lovers in places with great restaurants, such as Puerto Vallarta, Playa del Carmen, Mérida, Oaxaca and San Miguel de Allende. <b>Weak spot:</b> costs creep up with every meal and drink.</p>
<h3>Villa or vacation rental</h3>
<p>A house or condo with a kitchen, often with a private pool. <b>Best for:</b> larger groups and multi-generation families, long stays and people who like to cook or hire a local chef. <b>Weak spot:</b> you handle transport, groceries and problems yourself, and quality varies.</p>

<h2 id="numbers">Worked numbers</h2>
<p>Using the same baselines as our <a href="{{ root }}tools/trip-cost/">trip cost estimator</a> for a mid-range trip of seven nights at a typical $$ destination, before flights:</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="clim">
<thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>All-inclusive</th><th>Hotel + eating out</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Couple</td><td>about US$2,380</td><td>about US$1,470 + drinks</td><td style="text-align:left">Hotel wins if you don't drink much; all-inclusive wins if you do.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Family of four</td><td>about US$4,760</td><td>about US$2,940 + drinks and snacks</td><td style="text-align:left">Look for "kids stay free" deals, which can cut the all-inclusive price sharply.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eight friends</td><td>about US$9,520</td><td>A villa often beats both</td><td style="text-align:left">A four-bedroom villa with a cook can come in well below eight resort stays.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>These are planning figures, not quotes; seasons, brands and promotions move prices a lot. The point is the structure: all-inclusive pricing is per person, while rooms and villas are per unit, so groups favour rentals and light drinkers favour room-only hotels.</p>

<h2 id="hidden">The costs people forget</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>All-inclusive:</b> tips (staff appreciate them even when "included"), premium drinks and à la carte dinners, spa and motorised sports, airport transfers, and resort or environmental fees.</li>
<li><b>Hotels:</b> taxis or a rental car, beach-club minimum spends, and resort fees in some properties.</li>
<li><b>Villas:</b> cleaning fees, security deposits, utilities in long stays, a car (often essential), groceries at tourist prices, and staff tips.</li>
<li><b>Everywhere in Quintana Roo:</b> the state's Visitax fee per foreign visitor; see the <a href="{{ root }}guides/mexico-entry-requirements/">entry guide</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="decide">A five-question decision</h2>
<ol>
<li><b>Are there children under ten?</b> Lean all-inclusive, unless it's a villa with a pool for a big family.</li>
<li><b>Will you drink more than two or three drinks a day?</b> All-inclusive pays off.</li>
<li><b>Is the destination's food scene a reason to go?</b> Choose a hotel in town.</li>
<li><b>Are you six or more adults?</b> Price a villa first.</li>
<li><b>Do you want to explore every day?</b> A hotel or villa saves you paying for meals you won't eat.</li>
</ol>
<p>A good specialist can price all three for your dates in minutes. <a href="{{ root }}plan/">Get a free comparison</a>.</p>
